Hunting Artifacts
1. Check for a config archive at /var/netscaler/gui/vpn/c. An injected command runs tar czf /var/netscaler/gui/vpn/c -C /flash nsconfig, which tars up the entire /flash/nsconfig directory and drops it into the web-served /vpn/ folder. That means anyone, can download it at https://<your-gateway>/vpn/c.

2. Check /conf/httpd.conf for a webshell alias. Grep for this pattern: AliasMatch ^/logon/LogonPoint/css/LogonUISimple\.html\.style\.min\.[0-9a-f]+\.css$. This maps a CSS-looking URL to a webshell so it blends in with normal web traffic. If this line is in your httpd.conf, there's a webshell behind it.

3. Grep access logs for HTTP_NSC_CLIENTTYPE. Check /var/log/httperror.log and /var/log/httpaccess.log. This is the same type of injection as the username=anonymous payloads we already found, but attackers stuff shell commands into this HTTP header instead of the login field.

4. Grep for ===CONF: and ===KEY: in /var/netscaler/logon/LogonPoint/. Look inside any .css files in that directory, especially short random-named ones like 74tns8.css. Attackers are using these markers to dump stolen ns.conf content and SSL private keys into files that look like CSS to anyone glancing at a directory listing. 

5. Check /flash/nsconfig/ssl/ for stolen certs and keys. This directory holds the appliance's SSL certificates and private keys. Attackers are copying these alongside ns.conf.

6. Grep logs for the Platypus install token: plt_wqmnjp5jusrcpzicqa2t.gg3s7yppdptle5dyefxj. Check /var/log/ and /var/nslog/. This is the enrollment string the Platypus C2 agent uses to register with its server at entretiensol.com:443.
